{"type":"text","text":"performs the mutual-TLS handshake (wire schema "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"MtlsEndpointAliases"},{"type":"text","text":", contract 1.40; the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"seventh, CIBA’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"backchannel_authentication_endpoint"},{"type":"text","text":", joined in contract 1.58)."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MtlsEndpointAliases"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"anchor":"overview","text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A TLS listener decides whether to request a client certificate during the handshake,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"before it has seen any HTTP, so “ask for a certificate on "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\/oauth2\/token"},{"type":"text","text":" but not on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\/oauth2\/authorize"},{"type":"text","text":"” is not something one listener can do. A deployment wanting both runs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"two, and this object names the second."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Only these seven are ever aliased. ","type":"text"},{"code":"authorization_endpoint","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"end_session_endpoint","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"front-channel and ","type":"text"},{"code":"jwks_uri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is public key material, so §21.3 rule 2 forbids synthesising","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"an alias for any of them — sending a browser to an mTLS host raises a native","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"certificate-chooser dialog most users cannot answer. ","type":"text"},{"code":"issuer","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is not an endpoint and does","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"not move either: §12.4 rule 3 still compares ","type":"text"},{"code":"iss","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" against it by exact string.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every property is optional","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", though the server’s schema marks them required. AXIAM"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"builds them from one path through a shared macro and so always publishes the complete set,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"but RFC 8705 §5 permits an OP to alias fewer, and the shape of this member must never be"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"why a client stops working — the same principle rule 2 point 1 states for the object as a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"whole, one level in. A "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" entry falls back to the top-level endpoint of the same name,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"exactly as an absent object does."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/AxiamSDK\/documentation\/AxiamSDK\/MtlsEndpointAliases"},"references":{"doc://AxiamSDK/documentation/AxiamSDK/MtlsEndpointAliases/backchannelAuthenticationEndpoint":{"url":"\/documentation\/axiamsdk\/mtlsendpointaliases\/backchannelauthenticationendpoint","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"CIBA Core §7 — authenticates the client (contract 1.58, §21.3.1 vector A’s seventh"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"alias). A "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tls_client_auth"},{"type":"text","text":" CIBA client has no other way to present its certificate on a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"two-listener deployment."}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","title":"backchannelAuthenticationEndp